###################
# Help-Texts      #
###################

  
global {ODv_ArHelpText}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu File  
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagfile)} {NEW
Resets the program and the command ring

LOAD
Load a file with a SQL statement into the integrated editor

SAVE
Save result the contents of the editor window in a file. If no filename exist a filebox appears to specify one.

SAVE AS
The same function as SAVE with the difference that always a filebox appears to enter a filename

QUIT
Disconnect from the database and exit ODDIS
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu Options
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagoption)} {AUTOCOMMIT
Cascaded menu to change the autocommit function to ON or OFF

FONTSIZE
Cascaded menu to change the fontsize of all output windows

WINDOWS
Cascaded menu to provide the following display-options :
-One window per object (Previous window for this object is deleted.) 
-One window per relation (One for each different direction reaching this object, previous window for This relation is deleted.)   
-One window per query (For each request an own window, no window deleted).

DISPLAY
Cascaded menu to change the display type :
-Colour or grey
-Monochrome
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu SQL-Options
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagsqloptions)} {COMMIT NOW
Execute immediately a commit in the connected database

ROLLBACK
Execute immediately a rollback in the database

READ LONGFILE
Fetch the contents of a long field from the database and write it to a file

WRITE LONGFILE
Read the contents of a file and store it to a long field in the database

PROCEDURE EXEC
You can execute a PL/SQL-Block from the editor window. You can bind values to :variables

}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu User-Objects
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(taguserobjects)} {This menu represents the integrated queries to the data dictionary for ODDIS-users. You can select one item and get an output window which includes detailed information about the selected object owned by you. The queries use the USER-Views of the Oracle Data Dictionary. For further information on the queries itself refer to menu 'Help' and select the item USER-OBJECTS STRUCTURE.
}  

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu All-Objects
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagallobjects)} {This menu represents the integrated queries to the data dictionary for ODDIS-users. You can select one item and get an output window which includes detailed information about the selected database object which is accessible to you. The queries use the ALL-Views of the Oracle Data Dictinary. For further information on the queries itself refer to menu 'Help' and select the item ALL-OBJECTS STRUCTURE.
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu System
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagsystem)} {This menu represents the integrated
queries to the data dictionary for database-administrators. You can
select one item and get an output window which includes detailed
information about the selected database object. You need DBA rights to execute the queries of the system menu. The queries use the DBA-Views of the Oracle Data Dictionary. And you can create Device-Tables. For further information about the queries itself refer to menu 'Help' and select the item SYSTEM-STRUCTURE.
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menubutton Execute
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagexecute)} {Execute a SQL statement, which must be specified in the editor of the main window. The output of the statement is presented in the result window.
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menubutton Optimize
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(tagoptimize)} {
ANALYZE TABLES/INDEXES/CLUSTERS
Displays all user tables  (or indexes, clusters)  in a new window. You can select one object and get the actual ( or compute, estimate, delete)  statistics for it.

EXPLAIN 
Analyze a SQL statement, which must be specified in the editor of the main window and shows the execution plan of the statement in a special window. Here you can select each object in the execution plan and get more information about it. You can get the structure of the execution plan as graphic. And you can save and print the results.  
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS-Menu Help
  set {ODv_ArHelpText(taghelp)} {ORACLE
You have access to the Oracle help topics. If you select one topic you get detailed information about it in a new window.

USER-OBJECTS STRUCTURE
Displays the structure of the integrated queries to the data dictionary for objects owned by the user of ODDIS and gives detailed information for every query.

ALL-OBJECTS STRUCTURE
Displays the structure of the integrated queries to the data dictionary for all accessible objects to the user of ODDIS and gives detailed information for every query.

SYSTEM-STRUCTURE
Displays the structure of the integrated queries to the data dictionary and detailed information for every query. DBA-rights are needed.

ABOUT ODDIS
You get the information to which database you are connected to and your login name to the system and database. Also you get an overview of ODDIS and a short description of the functions.
}

# Used in Help-About ODDIS
global {ODv_helptext}
  set {ODv_helptext} {ODDIS - Overview

The Oracle Data Dictionary Information System (ODDIS) shows most of
the static information about the Data Dictionary of Oracle Version
7. You can click on a database object and the tool displays detailed
information about it in a formatted output window. Besides this you
have the possibility to edit a SQL statement in a special window. You
can load, save, and execute this statement. Also you can save and
print the results of the execution of the SQL statement.  ODDIS help
you to analyze user-objects and statements with an integrated analyze-
and explain- menu. You can get the structure of the plan as
graphic. Also you can save and print the results of the analysis of
the SQL statement. As DBA you can create new tables with information
about the devices. ODDIS provides an integrated access to the Oracle
help tables which contain a lot of topics and descriptions. Also ODDIS
shows you the menu structure of all integrated requests to the Data
Dictionary in a graphic. You can click on an item in the graphic and
get a short description including the SQL statement for the
request. In ODDIS you operate with the mouse and/or with the
keyboard. For a detailed description refer to the ODDIS manual.  The
next lines show the menus and buttons available from the main window
after ODDIS starts. You can click on one of the items to get further
information.  
}

# Used in SQL-Options-PLExec..
global {ODv_plexecinfo}
  set {ODv_plexecinfo} {PL/SQL Execute allows execution of the SQL text window with binding
of values to :variable names. The SQL text window may contain eithern
a complete PL/SQL block, or may be an anonzmous block to call 
a stored procedure (Version 7)
The Bind Variables entry should be used to bind values to the :variables.
Enter the name of a :variable (with leading colon) followed by the
value to bind. If a value contains spaces, place double quotas around
the value. If a :variable is output only, a dummy value is required.
For example, if the SQL window containd a call to a stored procedure
BEGIN
  assign_emp(:name, :age, :salary, :dept);
END;
then an appropriate entry might be
:name \\\"John Doe\\\"  :age 42  :salary 4.25  :dept \\\"Widget Mfg.\\\"
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in System-Device-Tables
global {ODv_sysdevtablesinfo}
  set {ODv_sysdevtablesinfo} {ORACLE-DEVICE-TABLES 

Before you can use the information about devices, 
you must create the tables. 
And you can give grants to roles or users.
Before insert into the device-tables,
you may delete the tables.
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in System-Device-Tables-Delete
global {ODv_sysdevicecreinfo}
  set {ODv_sysdevicecreinfo} {ORACLE-DEVICE-TABLES-CREATE 
Drop existing tables and creates two new tables:

information about devices with oracle mounts:

create table device$ (
device_id 	number(3) 	not null constraint oradev_pk primary key, 
fs_system 	varchar2(150) 	not null, 
blocks 		number(10) 	not null, 
used 		number(10) 	not null, 
free 		number(10) 	not null, 
percent 	number(3) 	not null, 
mount 		varchar2(150) 	not null)


information about devices and tablespaces:

create table device_dfile$ (
device_id 	number(3) 	not null constraint fk_devid references device$(device_id), 
tablespace_name varchar2(30) 	not null , 
file_id 	number(2) 	not null constraint devdat_pk primary key, 
file_name 	varchar2(257) 	not null)
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in System-Device-Tables-Grant
global {ODv_sysdevicegrainfo}
  set {ODv_sysdevicegrainfo} {ORACLE-DEVICE-TABLES-GRANT 
Grant select on the device tables to role or user, 
which you must specify.  
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in System-Device-Tables-Insert
global {ODv_sysdeviceinsinfo}
  set {ODv_sysdeviceinsinfo} {ORACLE-DEVICES-INSERT 

inserts all information about devices 
and inserts all information about devices and datafiles
into the device-tables 
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in System-Device-Tables-Delete
global {ODv_sysdevicedelinfo}
  set {ODv_sysdevicedelinfo} {ORACLE-DEVICES-DELETE 

removes all information about devices 
and removes all information about devices and datafiles 
from the device-tables.
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in Optimize-Analyze-Compute (Tables/Indexes/Clusters)
global {ODv_analyzecominfo}
  set {ODv_analyzecominfo} {ANALYZE-COMPUTE

Calculates each value. This option provides the 
most accurate statistics, but is the slowest to run.
For example to compute the statistics for a table, 
you issue the command as follows

analyze table <table_name> compute statistics.
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in Optimize-Analyze-Estimate (Tables/Indexes/Clusters)
global {ODv_analyzeestinfo}
  set {ODv_analyzeestinfo} {ANALYZE-ESTIMATE 

allows you to make estimate statistics by examining data dictionary 
values and performing data sampling. This provides less accurate 
statistics, but is much faster then compute.
For example, to analyze a table with 50 percent of rows, 
you issue the command as follow

analyze table <table_name> estimate statistics sample <50> percent.

To analyze an index  with 30 rows of rows, 
you issue the command as follows

analyze index <index_name> estimate statistics sample <30> rows.
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in Optimize-Analyze-Delete (Tables/Indexes/Clusters)
global {ODv_analyzedelinfo}
  set {ODv_analyzedelinfo} {ANALYZE-DELETE 

removes all table/index/cluster statistics (freeing space) 
for the specified object.
For example to delete the statistics for a table, 
you issue the command as follows

analyze table <table_name> delete statistics.
}

# Regine Kasten
# Used in Optimize-Explain
global {ODv_explainplaninfo}
  set {ODv_explainplaninfo} {OPERATIONS DISPLAYED IN EXPLAIN_PLAN OUTPUT

 Sign	Operation      Option      Description
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 AGG	AGGREGATE      GROUP BY    A single row that is the result of a GROUP BY (Version 7 only) 
 AND	AND-EQUAL                  Accepts multiple ROWIDs and returns the intersection of the sets with no duplicats
 CON	CONNECT BY                 Tree-walks rows using a CONNECT BY query
 CAT	CONCATENATION              Returns UNION all rows
 CNT	COUNTING                   Counts number of rows from a table
 FILT	FILTER                     Accepts some rows and eliminates others using WHERE conditions in statement
 FIRST	FIRST ROW                  Returns only first row of query
 FIXED  FIXED_TABLE                Accesses a FIXED TABLE
 UPD	FOR UPDATE                 Places row locks on selected rows
 IDX	INDEX          UNIQUE      Uses UNIQUE index (one row)
 IDX	INDEX          RANGE SCAN  Uses non-UNIQUE INDEX (more than one row)
 IDX	INDEX          RANGE SCAN  Same as above, but returns all rows 
 	               DESCENDING  in descending order
 INTER	INTERSECTION               Returns intersection rows (no duplicates)
 MJ	MERGE JOIN                 Gets two sets of rows, both sorted, and combines each row from one with the other
 MJ	MERGE JOIN     OUTER       Same as above, but with an outer join
 -	MINUS                      Uses MINUS function
 NL	NESTED LOOPS               Compares two sets  of rows and checks each row for a condition before returning data 
 NL	NESTED LOOPS   OUTER       Same as above, but includes outer join
 PRO	PROJECTION                 Returns a subset of columns from a table
 REM	REMOTE                     Uses database link to access another database
 SEQ	SEQUENCE                   Access a sequence
 SORT	SORT           UNIQUE      Sorts rows to eliminate duplicates
 SORT	SORT           GROUP BY    Sorts as result of a GROUP BY clause
 SORT	SORT           JOIN        Sorts rows prior to a merge join
 SORT	SORT           ORDER BY    Sorts as result of an ORDER BY clause
 TAB	TABLE_ACCESS   FULL        Performs a full-table scan
 TAB	TABLE_ACCESS   CLUSTER     Access a cluster
 TAB	TABLE_ACCESS   HASH        Uses hashing algorithm based on the key (Version 7 only)
 TAB	TABLE_ACCESS   ROWID       Accesses a table by its ROWID
 +	UNION-ALL                  Uses UNION statement
 VIEW	VIEW                       Does a retrieval from a virtuell table
}


